{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 130,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since the very beginning of quantum mechanics,\na debate has raged about how to interpret"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4350,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its bizarre predictions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6030,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And at the heart and origin of that debate\nis the quantum jump or quantum leap - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10260,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seemingly miraculous and instantaneous transitions\nof quantum systems that have always defied"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15540,
    "dDurationMs": 2719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observation or prediction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18259,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least, until now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24126,
    "dDurationMs": 3743,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The notion of a quantum jump or quantum leap\nis one of the founding concepts of quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27869,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28869,
    "dDurationMs": 3561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s really the OG of quantum weirdness\n- so much so that it’s become part of common"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32430,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lexicon, with very loose fidelity to the original\nmeaning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36150,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It comes from the idea that electrons in atoms\njump randomly and instantaneously from one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40540,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit or energy level to another, without\never occupying the intervening space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45670,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea has become so ingrained into how\nwe think about atoms that few think to question"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51290,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the notion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52290,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But one of the principal founders of quantum\nmechanics thought otherwise."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56310,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Erwin Schrodinger himself never accepted the\nidea of the quantum jump - but could also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61440,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never prove it wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62940,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That proof required precision measurements\nthat didn’t exist in Schrodinger’s time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67420,
    "dDurationMs": 5129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However they exist now - and the reality of\nthe quantum jump has finally been tested."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72549,
    "dDurationMs": 5321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It all started back in 1913, when the great\nDanish physicist Niels Bohr set about to explain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77870,
    "dDurationMs": 4819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emission spectra - the sharp bands of color\nproduced when a simple tube of gas is energized."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82689,
    "dDurationMs": 4991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He took a lead from the recent success of\nMax Planck, who in 1901 had managed to explain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87680,
    "dDurationMs": 5369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the colors of light produced by a heated object\n- the so-called blackbody spectrum - by assuming"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93049,
    "dDurationMs": 4731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that light is made up of irreducible packets\nof energy that we now call photons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97780,
    "dDurationMs": 5379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bohr placed a similar restriction on atoms\n- he required that electron energy levels"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103159,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were quantized - could only have very specific\nenergies that depended on the element."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109289,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electrons would then jump between energy levels\nby emitting or absorbing a photon that corresponded"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114359,
    "dDurationMs": 1930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the difference in energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116289,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The result was the Bohr model of the atom\n- the very first attempt at a quantum theory,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121319,
    "dDurationMs": 3561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it very neatly explained the specific\nfrequencies of light observed in emission"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124880,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spectra of hydrogen - although it failed for\nmore complex elements"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129030,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bohr’s work inspired Werner Heisenberg and\nErwin Schrodinger to develop the first complete"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134940,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formulations of quantum mechanics in 1925."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138180,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultimately, these successfully predicted the\nspectra of elements of any complexity - and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143690,
    "dDurationMs": 1640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much more besides."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145330,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But one thing remained mysterious - what was\nactually happening during the quantum jump,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151080,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and what determined when a quantum jump would\noccur?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154010,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To address questions like this, Bohr and Heisenberg\nteamed up to develop the “Copenhagen interpretation”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160020,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161420,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Copenhagen describes transitions in quantum\nstates as fundamentally random - the dice"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166080,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are rolled, and the system transitions instantaneously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170660,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The electron goes from one energy level to\nthe other without moving in between."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175530,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Copenhagen says that “measurement collapses\nthe wavefunction”, which these days is more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180730,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "often taken to mean that interaction with\nthe environment causes the state transition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185370,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- causes the quantum jump."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187930,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But one guy was not impressed by this idea\n- the inventor of the wavefunction himself,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193300,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Erwin Schrödinger."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194980,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When he visited Bohr’ home in 1926, Schrödinger\nsaid “If we are still going to have to put"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200720,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up with these damn quantum jumps, I am sorry\nthat I ever had anything to do with quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206190,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207190,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Schrödinger became bed-ridden with an illness\nduring that visit - perhaps he was that sick"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210870,
    "dDurationMs": 1310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212180,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it didn’t help - supposedly Bohr continued\nharanguing the poor sick man with the Copenhagen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217300,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "world-view."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218300,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bohr’s efforts were to no avail."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220560,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1952, Schrödinger published a two-part\nessay titled “Are there quantum jumps?”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225870,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wherein he compared the theory of quantum\njumps to that of epicycles—the long dead"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230540,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory about the motion of the planets in\nan Earth-centered solar system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234650,
    "dDurationMs": 5011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He claimed that both epicycles and quantum\njumps were ﻿“ingenious constructs of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239661,
    "dDurationMs": 5289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "human mind” that nevertheless were not true\ndescriptions of nature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244950,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why did Schrodinger hate quantum jumps\nso much?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247710,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Simply put, they seemed unnatural and unphysical\n- a hack added to cover up a phenomenon that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253590,
    "dDurationMs": 3050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum theory could not yet properly explain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256640,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The debate over quantum jumps was just one\npart of the larger discussion about the quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261580,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nature of reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263200,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was the Copenhagen interpretation versus\n- well “not-Copenhagen”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267780,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most famous quote on the not-Copenhagen\nside was from Albert Einstein - “God does"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272720,
    "dDurationMs": 2070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not play dice with nature”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274790,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was a reaction against one of the central\ntenants of Copenhagen - that subatomic phenomena"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279990,
    "dDurationMs": 3070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are fundamentally random, or probabilistic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283060,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Schrodinger had his arguments too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285660,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He believed it all came down to waves—and\nthat nothing was particularly special about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290990,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these waves compared to any other kind of\nclassical wave."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294270,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He argued that most “spooky” quantum phenomena\ncould be explained by classical resonance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300370,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "phenomena."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301370,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He rejected the idea of the “photon” as\nan irreducible energy packet, and even dismissed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305880,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the notion that electrons transitioned between\ndiscrete energy levels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310070,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He argued that the same emission spectra could\nbe got by thinking of these levels as fundamental"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315350,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vibrational modes, like on a drum or guitar\nstring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319250,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An atomic electron could then be considered\na superposition of multiple vibrational modes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324570,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that meant the electron could transition\nsmoothly through a series of intermediate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328460,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states during each transition, rather than\nundergoing instantaneous quantum jumps."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334700,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To Schrodinger, a big part of the problem\nwas that Bohr and others were using the behavior"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339210,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of systems of many, many individual particles\nto infer the behavior of individual particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344970,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He believed that it was completely nonsensical\nto even think about single particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349670,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As he put it, “﻿we never experiment with\njust one electron or atom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354220,
    "dDurationMs": 5919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In thought-experiments we sometimes assume\nthat we do; this invariably entails ridiculous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360139,
    "dDurationMs": 2301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consequences…”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362440,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That was in 1952 - and in 1952 we had never\nseen a single photon produced by a single"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368940,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum jump in a single atom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371540,
    "dDurationMs": 3730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in time we figured out how to do exactly\nthat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375270,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fast forward 30 years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376830,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the 80s we’d learned how to trap and\ncool a single atom with lasers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381090,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in 1986, almost simultaneously, three\ndifferent teams observed quantum jumps in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386320,
    "dDurationMs": 1099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "such an atom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387419,
    "dDurationMs": 1470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s how it worked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388889,
    "dDurationMs": 5041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The single atom - in this case mercury or\nbarium - is bathed in a laser beam with a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393930,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frequency exactly tuned to the energy difference\nbetween two of its electron levels - call"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399480,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them 1 and 2."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401220,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the electron is in level 1, it should jump\nto level 2 by absorbing a photon from the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405360,
    "dDurationMs": 1290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "laser light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406650,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the electron then falls back to level 1\nit should emit an identical photon in a random"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411340,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412340,
    "dDurationMs": 3549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the right choice of energy levels, this\nshould happen extremely quickly - the electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415889,
    "dDurationMs": 3661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should become locked between the two levels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419550,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the 1986 experiments, the electron in the\ntrapped atom jumped between levels something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424500,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like 100 million times per second."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426770,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The individual photons emitted in this process\ncouldn’t be seen - instead the single atom"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431800,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just glowed, or fluoresced."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434620,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This wasn’t a direct observation of individual\nquantum jumps - that required an extra level"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439970,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of cleverness, as well as an extra energy\nlevel - we’ll call this level 3."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445710,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Level 3 is far more stable than level 2 - an\nelectron that finds itself there may take"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450850,
    "dDurationMs": 2530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many seconds to drop back down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453380,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, we have our atom happily fluorescing in\nthe original laser beam."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457670,
    "dDurationMs": 4999,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now flash a second laser with frequency tuned\nto take the electron from level 1 to level"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462669,
    "dDurationMs": 1051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463720,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Suddenly the atom goes dark - the fluorescence\nstops, because the electron is stuck in level"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468820,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3 and no longer available to cycle between\n1 and 2."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472580,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then, after a period of time, the electron\ndecays and fluorescence starts again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478050,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this way, physicists were able to gain\nfairly direct evidence of a single quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481860,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "jump."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482860,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the downward jumps when the electron decayed\nout of level 3 appeared to occur at completely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487210,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "random times."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488770,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just as Bohr had predicted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490490,
    "dDurationMs": 1610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, that settled it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492100,
    "dDurationMs": 2090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Schrödinger was wrong and Bohr was right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494190,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495190,
    "dDurationMs": 1430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not so fast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496620,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although the jump appeared random, there was\nno way to tell whether it was instantaneous,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501370,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or whether the electron passed through some\nintermediate states during the jump"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506120,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fast forward another 30+ years to, well, a\nyear ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510650,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Technology has advanced to the point that\nwe can not only see individual quantum jumps"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515740,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- we can monitor their progress, and even\ninterrupt them mid-jump."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519810,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now this isn’t with an actual atom, but\nrather a sort of “artificial atom made of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526270,
    "dDurationMs": 2020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two superconducting circuits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528290,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The 3 different energy levels of this artificial\natom corresponded to the number of electromagnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533380,
    "dDurationMs": 3170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quanta of energy stored in the circuits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536550,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ground state (or state 1 using the notation\nfrom the previous experiment) corresponded"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541680,
    "dDurationMs": 6930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to zero quanta in either circuit, states 2\nand 3 corresponded to 1 quantum in either"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548610,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "respective circuit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549750,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They then placed these artificial atoms inside\na microwave cavity - analogous to the laser,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556050,
    "dDurationMs": 3470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which could cause the “atom” to transition\nbetween states."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559520,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this also allowed the researchers to monitor\nthe state of the system with far greater resolution"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565220,
    "dDurationMs": 3470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than in the 1986 experiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568690,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They could actually zoom in on a quantum jump\nand finally figure out whether it truly was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574000,
    "dDurationMs": 1810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an instantaneous transition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575810,
    "dDurationMs": 7730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They found that … no, it was not instantaneous\nafter all, but rather was a continuous transition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583540,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over intermediate states that took a few microseconds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587540,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that transition appeared to be perfectly\ndescribed by theory - in this case quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593560,
    "dDurationMs": 2010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trajectory theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595570,
    "dDurationMs": 2170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about the randomness of the event?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597740,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the spacing between events did appear\nto be random, as Bohr thought."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602420,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But just prior to each jump the system started\nto shift in a way that enabled the researchers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608420,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to predict the oncoming jump."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611300,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that ability to predict also allowed them\nto reverse the quantum jumps midflight by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616750,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "adjusting the microwave field during the process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619680,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Given that the quantum jump onset was predictable,\nand that its trajectory was extremely well"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624190,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "described by theoretical calculations, it’s\ntempting to wonder if the whole process is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628700,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "driven by an underlying deterministic mechanism,\nrather than fundamental randomness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633840,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I suspect both Schrodinger and Einstein would\nagree."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637720,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what’s really going on here?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640460,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, just a couple of months ago, a group\nof theorists claim to have taken a major step"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645240,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "towards figuring it out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647020,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They explain this non-instantaneous quantum\ntransition in terms of something called the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651930,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum Zeno Effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654520,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We'll need a full episode to explain this\nphenomenon properly, but in short, the act"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658980,
    "dDurationMs": 6230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of measuring a system will, in Copenhagen\nterms, collapse the wavefunction, which drastically"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665210,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "changes how the system behaves - for example,\ntrapping the system in one state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670120,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The theorists showed how quantum states can\ntransition very predictably through a series"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675120,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of superposition states - much as Schrodinger\nproposed - but in addition to these predictable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679910,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum jumps there are fundamentally unpredictable\nones--truly unpredictable and sudden quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683910,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "jumps that would've made Bohr proud."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686310,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the difference?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687310,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's to do with how strongly the system is\ncoupled to the measurement apparatus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691080,
    "dDurationMs": 4819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The weaker the measurement, the less likely\na true quantum jump is to occur."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695899,
    "dDurationMs": 1821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But more on that another time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697720,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now, both the Bohr and Schrodinger camps\nhave new evidence in their favour."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704370,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the longest time, physicists have shied\naway from asking which interpretation of quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708750,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mechanics is correct."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710110,
    "dDurationMs": 4659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s considered by many to be a point of\nphilosophical preference whether you roll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714769,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dice with Bohr and Heisenberg in Copenhagen,\nor ride the continuous and deterministic wave"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720149,
    "dDurationMs": 2161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Schrodinger and others."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722310,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But experimentalists are proving far cleverer\nthan the naysayers imagined."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726500,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nearly a century after Bohr and Schrodinger\nstarted the argument, we may be on the verge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730790,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the next quantum leap - to learning whether\nquantum jumps are instantaneous or continuous,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736720,
    "dDurationMs": 5369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and perhaps even whether the quantum world\nis built upon fundamentally random processes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742089,
    "dDurationMs": 7245,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or is driven by rigidly deterministic mechanic\nof space time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
